# Cloudflare Pages redirects. Copied verbatim out of public/ at build time.
#
# /building/ was live and indexed from July 29-30, 2026. /builds/ and
# /builds/<id>/ were the next door, live from July 30. Those URLs exist in
# Google's index and in anything anyone shared, so they redirect rather
# than 404.
#
# August 24, 2026: the house is In Your Sight. URL stays /writing/.
#
# 301 (permanent) is deliberate: it passes ranking signal to the new URL and
# tells Google to replace the old one in its index. A 302 would leave the dead
# URL indexed indefinitely.
#
# Note on fragments: browsers never send the "#personal-ai-os" part to the
# server, so no redirect rule can route it. That is handled on the other end —
# /writing/ keeps an id for each former build, so an old deep link lands in
# the house instead of 404ing.
/building/                 /writing/  301
/building                  /writing/  301
/builds/                   /writing/  301
/builds                    /writing/  301
/builds/personal-ai-os/    /writing/  301
/builds/personal-ai-os     /writing/  301
/builds/llm-wiki/          /writing/  301
/builds/llm-wiki           /writing/  301
/builds/kylecovan-com/     /writing/  301
/builds/kylecovan-com      /writing/  301
#
# August 29, 2026: story essay taken down (draft). The URL was live briefly —
# redirect into the house rather than 404.
/writing/new-direction-reflections/  /writing/  301
/writing/new-direction-reflections   /writing/  301
#
# August 30, 2026: three early posts taken off the blog (draft). Same pattern —
# URLs were live; redirect into the house rather than 404.
/writing/too-many-ideas/                        /writing/  301
/writing/too-many-ideas                         /writing/  301
/writing/startup-operations-now-ai-orchestration/  /writing/  301
/writing/startup-operations-now-ai-orchestration   /writing/  301
/writing/four-ai-operating-systems/             /writing/  301
/writing/four-ai-operating-systems              /writing/  301
#
# Conventional /sitemap.xml → Astro's sitemap-index.xml. robots.txt already
# points at the real file; this catches tools and humans that still request the
# short name. Same pattern as tapocanyon.com. (Kept from main, August 12 QA.)
/sitemap.xml  /sitemap-index.xml  301
